@meta
id: 12-15
title: Delete the match as well
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-offset, search-motion
par: 40
minutes: 6
vscode: select the two columns and their commas, carefully, and delete
open: export.columns.ts

@brief
The order export still carries two columns from the system that was
retired last year. `legacyRef` and `legacySource` come out of the header,
along with the commas that separate them, and nothing else changes.

    before: id,customerId,legacyRef,legacySource,status,total,...
    after:  id,customerId,status,total,...

A plain search motion stops *before* what it found, which was the right
behaviour in the last few lessons and is the wrong behaviour here: the
comma after `legacySource` has to go too, and it is the last character of
the thing you would naturally search for.

An offset fixes it, and it fixes it by changing the kind of motion the
search is.

@keys
| `d/text/e` `Enter` | delete up to **and including** the match |
| `d/text` `Enter` | delete up to but not including it |
| `d/text/e+1` `Enter` | and one character further |

@hints
One delete, not two. Start at the first character that should disappear
and describe the last one.
--
`d/pattern` would stop just before the match. Adding the `/e` offset
makes the motion inclusive, so the match itself is deleted as well.
--
`/legacyRef` `Enter` to get to the start of the doomed text, then
`d/legacySource,/e` `Enter` — the pattern ends with the comma, and `/e`
means that comma is the last thing deleted.

@solution
/legacyRef<Enter>  d/legacySource,/e<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-three keystrokes, and the two ends of the deletion were described
rather than aimed at.

The rule underneath is worth memorising because it is not obvious: a
search motion is exclusive, but a search motion *with an `e` offset* is
inclusive. `d/foo` deletes up to `foo`; `d/foo/e` deletes up to and
through `foo`. That single letter is the difference between leaving a
stray comma behind and not.

This is the most useful pairing in the chapter. Real deletions rarely
stop neatly at a word boundary — they stop after a closing tag, after a
semicolon, after the end of a marker comment. `d/marker/e` covers all of
those, and `d/marker/e+1` covers the one where you want the character
after it too.

Worth trying once, on your own time: `d/pattern/-1` and `d/pattern/+1`.
An offset with a bare number counts *lines*, not characters, and it turns
the motion linewise — `d/pattern/+1` deletes whole lines, from this one
down to the line after the match. That is the shape you want when you are
cutting a block out of a config file, and it is the last thing this
chapter has to give you.

@start export.columns.ts
export const ORDER_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,customerId,legacyRef,legacySource,status,total,currency,placedAt,shippedAt';

export const CUSTOMER_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,email,name,country,createdAt,marketingOptIn';

export const PAYOUT_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,batch,orderId,amount,fee,net,scheduledAt,sentAt';

export const REFUND_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,orderId,amount,reason,approvedBy,approvedAt';

export const exportHeaders = {
  orders: ORDER_EXPORT_HEADER,
  customers: CUSTOMER_EXPORT_HEADER,
  payouts: PAYOUT_EXPORT_HEADER,
  refunds: REFUND_EXPORT_HEADER,
};

@expected export.columns.ts
export const ORDER_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,customerId,status,total,currency,placedAt,shippedAt';

export const CUSTOMER_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,email,name,country,createdAt,marketingOptIn';

export const PAYOUT_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,batch,orderId,amount,fee,net,scheduledAt,sentAt';

export const REFUND_EXPORT_HEADER =
  'id,orderId,amount,reason,approvedBy,approvedAt';

export const exportHeaders = {
  orders: ORDER_EXPORT_HEADER,
  customers: CUSTOMER_EXPORT_HEADER,
  payouts: PAYOUT_EXPORT_HEADER,
  refunds: REFUND_EXPORT_HEADER,
};
